---
import { site } from "../../site";
import SectionLabel from "../SectionLabel.astro";
---

<section class="section" id="about" aria-labelledby="closing-heading">
  <div class="wrap split">
    <div>
      <SectionLabel index="05" label="About" />
      <h2 id="closing-heading">About</h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="lead">
        I like systems with a shape you can explain out loud. Explicit
        interfaces, boundaries you can point at, and failure modes that show up
        where they were caused rather than three layers away.
      </p>
      <p class="muted">
        Most of my work is Go on the backend and Kotlin on Android, with React
        and TypeScript when something needs a browser, and Linux, Docker and CI
        in the parts nobody sees. The problems I keep coming back to are
        networking ones: service discovery, realtime channels, and deciding
        exactly what two programs are allowed to say to each other.
      </p>
      <p class="section-more">
        <a class="more-link" href="/about/">How I work →</a>
      </p>

      <div id="contact" class="closing__contact">
        <p class="meta">Contact</p>
        <p class="muted closing__contact-note">
          Email is best. I am most useful on backend systems, mobile clients,
          and the networking and infrastructure around them.
        </p>
        <ul class="contact-list">
          <li>
            <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>
              <span>Email</span>
              <span>{site.email}</span>
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={site.github} rel="noopener noreferrer">
              <span>GitHub</span>
              <span>gabriel-kimutai</span>
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={site.linkedin} rel="noopener noreferrer">
              <span>LinkedIn</span>
              <span>gabriel-kimutai</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
